veloper/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-165042" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(e,\\frac{5}{4})$ and $({e}^{-2},\\frac{1}{2})$"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/state.json" "file_sha256" "650416bffcabe436ad78a349440d52b26e024954aa80b568c20859a03b60249d" "basis" 1724 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-12567" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a tangent vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=t\\,\\mathbf i+t^2\\,\\mathbf j+t^3\\,\\mathbf k$ at the point $P(1,1,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/example-12567.json" "file_sha256" "a4e0f7afc54eedd8b18b64741f483749958ec3f0efc37e78363e54f6c281daec" "basis" 1724 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-12567" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The point $P(1,1,1)$ is at the tip of $\\mathbf r(t)$ for some specific value $t_0$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point,  the vector $\\mathbf r'(t_0)$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\,i+\\,j+\\,k \\\\ t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+{t}^{3}\\,k & =i+\\,j+\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf i, \\mathbf j,$ and $\\mathbf k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}t=1 \\\\ {t}^{2}=1 \\\\ {t}^{3}=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=1.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,1,1)$ is at the tip of the vector $\\mathbf r(1).$  \n\nNow, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,k \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =i+2(1)\\,j+3(1)^{2}\\,k \\\\  & =i+2\\,j+3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at at the point $P(1,1,1)$ is $\\,\\mathbf i+2\\,\\mathbf j+3\\,\\mathbf k.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/assets/bae639af7c4267014af4e558f9a21b042470a3f7d46fa7a31ac0162c7b08ef08.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/example-12567.json" "file_sha256" "a4e0f7afc54eedd8b18b64741f483749958ec3f0efc37e78363e54f6c281daec" "basis" 1724 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-12568" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a parametrization of the tangent line to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle \\cos{t}, \\: \\sin{t},\\: 4t^2\\right\\rangle$ at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/example-12568.json" "file_sha256" "847c584ec03c3122192fcd170e3728c29c404d293f654ecc6dd141379adf1cf6" "basis" 1724 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-12568" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let  $P$ be the point at the tip of the vector $\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right ).$  At this point,  the vector $\\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right )$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(u)=\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right )+u \\, \\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right ), \\qquad u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,4{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}⟩=⟨0,1,{\\pi }^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,8t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,8(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,4\\pi ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the given curve at the point $P$ where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(u)=⟨0,1,{\\pi }^{2}⟩+u⟨-1,0,4\\pi ⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/assets/e24b811fb8d9e43bd0682e3686f8b2ef1198e36016978ca305a7df4dffe21889.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/example-12568.json" "file_sha256" "847c584ec03c3122192fcd170e3728c29c404d293f654ecc6dd141379adf1cf6" "basis" 1724 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-12580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t^2+1\\,,\\, 6t+3\\right\\rangle,$ find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1\\right\\rangle.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/example-12580.json" "file_sha256" "4eef03666effb06cc7ab81ab916550691d098899a1682df4b04f56d030b7dd88" "basis" 1724 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-12580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1)\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t+3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t\\,,\\,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors  $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1 \\right\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf r'(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1 \\right\\rangle \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) = 0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨3\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨3\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨2t\\,,\\,6⟩ & =0 \\\\ 3\\cdot 2t+1\\cdot 6 & =0 \\\\ 6t+6 & =0 \\\\ t & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=-1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨(-1)^{2}+1\\,,\\,6(-1)+3⟩=⟨2\\,,\\,-3⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1\\right\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf r'(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(2,-3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/example-12580.json" "file_sha256" "4eef03666effb06cc7ab81ab916550691d098899a1682df4b04f56d030b7dd88" "basis" 1724 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-12569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=(t^2+t)\\,\\mathbf i+(t^2-t)\\,\\mathbf j,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/example-12569.json" "file_sha256" "86b1cc6461fc7afc37bc987047b25162e2c937795160255649aec7f8cb8483ed" "basis" 1724 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-12569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,j \\\\  & =(2t+1)\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $\\mathbf r'(t)=c(3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2t+1)\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j=c(3\\,i+\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ gives the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2t+1=3c \\\\ 2t-1=c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $c=2t-1.$ Substituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t+1 & =3c \\\\ 2t+1 & =3(2t-1) \\\\ 2t+1 & =6t-3 \\\\ 6t-2t & =1+3 \\\\ 4t & =4 \\\\ t & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf r'$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =({1}^{2}+1)\\,i+({1}^{2}-1)\\,j=2\\,i,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel at the point $(2,0).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/assets/0f723e89b1c91367d6488e034f8b627b3c406aed6d8d1ffc244d6f014021e04e.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/example-12569.json" "file_sha256" "86b1cc6461fc7afc37bc987047b25162e2c937795160255649aec7f8cb8483ed" "basis" 1724 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-126835" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨3t+5,1-{t}^{3}⟩$ at the point where $t=0,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/state.json" "file_sha256" "10e5437f5152fd7bd9ea7204ccaaeb316ddab2841627e94fc13e3b5d4af280e2" "basis" 2098 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-126835" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "At the point where $t=0,$ the vector ${r}^{′}(0)$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n \n\n$$\nR(u)=r(0)+u{r}^{′}(0),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨3(0)+5,1-{0}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+5),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0) & =⟨3,-3(0)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=⟨5,1⟩+u⟨3,0⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/state.json" "file_sha256" "10e5437f5152fd7bd9ea7204ccaaeb316ddab2841627e94fc13e3b5d4af280e2" "basis" 2098 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-126835" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0bf882978d8b075256a354c0dd5eacd4cca6c4758827f5db5d0ae9f7f9e00a35" "basis" 2098 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-126835" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/state.json" "file_sha256" "10e5437f5152fd7bd9ea7204ccaaeb316ddab2841627e94fc13e3b5d4af280e2" "basis" 2098 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-126835" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨3,-1⟩+u⟨1,5⟩"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/state.json" "file_sha256" "10e5437f5152fd7bd9ea7204ccaaeb316ddab2841627e94fc13e3b5d4af280e2" "basis" 2098 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-