my/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-126741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a vector that's tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{3}-1,{t}^{2}+1⟩$ at the point where $t=-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-126741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "At the point where $t=-2,$ the vector ${r}^{′}(-2)$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nLet's calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2},2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(-2) & =⟨3(-2)^{2},2(-2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=-2$ is $⟨12,-4⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-126741" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "943d99b88dd5ef61db0015339755f4f0ea9e8b2c195300e13bfb7ee419247aea"} {"question" "q-126741" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-126741" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-6,-3⟩"] ["math" "⟨-6,2⟩"] ["math" "⟨12,-4⟩"] ["math" "⟨12,2⟩"] ["math" "⟨3,-4⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-126741" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨12,-4⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-126740" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=2\\sqrt{3-t}\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j+(t+3)\\,k$ at the point $P(2,-3,5).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-126740" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The point $P(2,-3,5)$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point, the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2\\,i-3\\,j+5\\,k \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3-t}\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j+(t+3)\\,k & =2\\,i-3\\,j+5\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3-t}=2 \\\\ 1-2t=-3 \\\\ t+3=5\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we get $t=2.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(2,-3,5)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(2).$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{3-t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+3)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3-t}}\\,i-2\\,j+k \\\\ {r}^{′}(2) & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3-2}}\\,i-2\\,j+k \\\\  & =-\\,i-2\\,j+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point $P(2,-3,5)$ is $-\\,i-2\\,j+k.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-126740" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "943d99b88dd5ef61db0015339755f4f0ea9e8b2c195300e13bfb7ee419247aea"} {"question" "q-126740" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-126740" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2\\,i-3\\,j+k"] ["math" "-\\,i+j+3\\,k"] ["math" "-\\,i-2\\,j+k"] ["math" "2\\,i-2\\,j+k"] ["math" "\\,i+j-2\\,k"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-126740" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\,i-2\\,j+k"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-126739" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=(3t+2)\\,i+(6t+1)\\,j+(1-4{t}^{2})\\,k$ at the point $P(2,1,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-126739" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The point $P(2,1,1)$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point, the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =2\\,i+j+k \\\\ (3t+2)\\,i+(6t+1)\\,j+(1-4{t}^{2})\\,k & =2\\,i+j+k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3t+2=2 \\\\ 6t+1=1 \\\\ 1-4{t}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=0.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(2,1,1)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(0).$\n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+2)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t+1)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-4{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =3\\,i+6\\,j-8t\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =3\\,i+6\\,j-8(0)\\,k \\\\  & =3\\,i+6\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point $P(2,1,1)$ is $3\\,i+6\\,j.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-126739" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "943d99b88dd5ef61db0015339755f4f0ea9e8b2c195300e13bfb7ee419247aea"} {"question" "q-126739" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-126739" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\,i+\\,j"] ["math" "2\\,i+\\,j+\\,k"] ["math" "3\\,i+6\\,j"] ["math" "3\\,i-8\\,k"] ["math" "6\\,i+4\\,j-3\\,k"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-126739" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3\\,i+6\\,j"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-75199" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a tangent vector to the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{-t},\\,{t}^{2}⟩$ at the point where $t=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-75199" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "At the point where $t=0$,  the vector ${r}^{′}(0)$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nLet's calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{e}^{t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{e}^{-t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,-{e}^{-t},\\,2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨{e}^{0},\\,-{e}^{-0},\\,2\\cdot 0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=0$ is $⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-75199" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "943d99b88dd5ef61db0015339755f4f0ea9e8b2c195300e13bfb7ee419247aea"} {"question" "q-75199" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-75199" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨0,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩"] ["math" "⟨0,\\,1,\\,-1⟩"] ["math" "⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩"] ["math" "⟨1,\\,0,\\,-1⟩"] ["math" "⟨1,\\,1,\\,0⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-75199" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨1,\\,-1,\\,0⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-32916" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a vector that's tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨2(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,2(t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩$ at the point where $t=\\pi .$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-32916" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "At the point where $t=\\pi $,  the vector ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nLet's calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[2(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)],\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[2(t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)]⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,2(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ),\\,2(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2(1-(-1)),\\,2(1+0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at the point where $t=\\pi $  is  $⟨4,\\,2⟩$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-32916" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "943d99b88dd5ef61db0015339755f4f0ea9e8b2c195300e13bfb7ee419247aea"} {"question" "q-32916" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-32916" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-2,\\,2⟩"] ["math" "⟨2,\\,2⟩"] ["math" "⟨4,\\,-2⟩"] ["math" "⟨4,\\,2⟩"] ["math" "⟨4,\\,4⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-32916" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨4,\\,2⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-126841" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+1),1-5t⟩$ at the point where $t=0,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-126841" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "At the point where $t=0,$ the vector ${r}^{′}(0)$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n \n\n$$\nR(u)=r(0)+u{r}^{′}(0),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡({0}^{2}+1),1-5(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({t}^{2}+1)),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-5t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}+1},-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0) & =⟨\\frac{2(0)}{{0}^{2}+1},-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-5⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=⟨0,1⟩+u⟨0,-5⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-126841" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "943d99b88dd5ef61db0015339755f4f0ea9e8b2c195300e13bfb7ee419247aea"} {"question" "q-126841" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-126841" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨0,-5⟩"] ["math" "⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨2,0⟩"] ["math" "⟨0,1⟩+u⟨0,-5⟩"] ["math" "⟨0,1⟩+u⟨2,-5⟩"] ["math" "⟨0,1⟩+u⟨2,0⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-126841" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨0,1⟩+u⟨0,-5⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-33450" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=t\\,i+({t}^{3}+1)\\,j+{t}^{5}\\,k$ at the point $P(1,2,1),$ \nvalid for real values of $u?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-33450" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The point $P(1,2,1)$ is at the tip of $r(t)$ for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point,  the vector ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nR(u)=r({t}_{0})+u\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =i+2\\,j+k \\\\ t\\,i+({t}^{3}+1)\\,j+{t}^{5}\\,k & =i+2\\,j+k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}t=1 \\\\ {t}^{3}+1=2 \\\\ {t}^{5}=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=1.$ This value also satifies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,2,1)$ is at the tip of the vector $r(1).$  \n\nWe calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+1)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{5})\\,k \\\\  & =i+3{t}^{2}\\,j+5{t}^{4}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(1) & =i+3(1)^{2}\\,j+5(1)^{4}\\,k \\\\  & =i+3\\,j+5\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point $P(1,2,1)$ is \n\n$$\nR(u)=i+2\\,j+\\,k+u(\\,i+3\\,j+5\\,k),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-33450" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "943d99b88dd5ef61db0015339755f4f0ea9e8b2c195300e13bfb7ee419247aea"} {"question" "q-33450" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-33450" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "i+2\\,j+\\,k+u(\\,i+3\\,j+4\\,k)"] ["math" "i+2\\,j+\\,k+u(\\,i+3\\,j+5\\,k)"] ["math" "i+2\\,j+\\,k+u(\\,i+4\\,j+5\\,k)"] ["math" "i+2\\,j+\\,k+u(\\,i+\\,j+4\\,k)"] ["math" "i+\\,j+\\,k+u(\\,i+3\\,j+5\\,k)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-33450" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "i+2\\,j+\\,k+u(\\,i+3\\,j+5\\,k)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-126838" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ at the point where $t=\\pi ,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-126838" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "At the point where $t=\\pi ,$ the vector ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n \n\n$$\nR(u)=r(\\pi )+u{r}^{′}(\\pi ),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point where $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨0,1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-126838" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "943d99b88dd5ef61db0015339755f4f0ea9e8b2c195300e13bfb7ee419247aea"} {"question" "q-126838" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-126838" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨-1,0⟩"] ["math" "⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨-1,1⟩"] ["math" "⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨0,1⟩"] ["math" "⟨1,1⟩+u⟨-1,0⟩"] ["math" "⟨1,1⟩+u⟨0,1⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-126838" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨-1,0⟩+u⟨0,1⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-126835" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨3t+5,1-{t}^{3}⟩$ at the point where $t=0,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-126835" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "At the point where $t=0,$ the vector ${r}^{′}(0)$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n \n\n$$\nR(u)=r(0)+u{r}^{′}(0),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨3(0)+5,1-{0}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+5),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0) & =⟨3,-3(0)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=⟨5,1⟩+u⟨3,0⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-126835" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "943d99b88dd5ef61db0015339755f4f0ea9e8b2c195300e13bfb7ee419247aea"} {"question" "q-126835" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-126835" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨3,-1⟩+u⟨1,5⟩"] ["math" "⟨3,-1⟩+u⟨3,0⟩"] ["math" "⟨5,1⟩+u⟨1,5⟩"] ["math" "⟨5,1⟩+u⟨3,-3⟩"] ["math" "⟨5,1⟩+u⟨3,0⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-126835" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨5,1⟩+u⟨3,0⟩"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-33461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+{sin}^{2}t\\,k$ at the point corresponding to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-33461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "At the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$,  the vector ${r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n \n\n$$\nR(u)=r(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+u{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j+{sin}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,k \\\\  & =\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({sin}^{2}t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,j-\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})k \\\\  & =\\,i-\\,j+\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point \n where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(\\,i-\\,j+\\,k),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-33461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "943d99b88dd5ef61db0015339755f4f0ea9e8b2c195300e13bfb7ee419247aea"} {"question" "q-33461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-33461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(-\\,i+2\\,j+\\,k)"] ["math" "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(2\\,i-\\,j+\\,k)"] ["math" "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(\\,i+2\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k)"] ["math" "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(\\,i-\\,j+\\,k)"] ["math" "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(i-\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-33461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\,i+\\,j+\\frac{1}{2}\\,k+u(\\,i-\\,j+\\,k)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-164834" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+1\\,,\\,2t-1⟩,$ find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $⟨1\\,,\\,-2⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-164834" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1)\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t\\,,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors  $⟨1\\,,\\,-2⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $⟨1\\,,\\,-2⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨1\\,,\\,-2⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨1\\,,\\,-2⟩\\cdot ⟨2t\\,,\\,2⟩ & =0 \\\\ 1\\cdot 2t+(-2)\\cdot 2 & =0 \\\\ 2t-4 & =0 \\\\ 2(t-2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=2.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨{(2)}^{2}+1\\,,\\,2(2)-1⟩=⟨5\\,,\\,3⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨1\\,,\\,-2⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(5,3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-164834" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "943d99b88dd5ef61db0015339755f4f0ea9e8b2c195300e13bfb7ee419247aea"} {"question" "q-164834" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-164834" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(1,-1)"] ["math" "(2,-3)"] ["math" "(5,3)"] ["text" "$(1,-1)$ and $(5,3)$"] ["text" "$(2,-3)$ and $(10,5)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-164834" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(5,3)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-126898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}+2t,4-{t}^{2}⟩,$ find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $⟨0,-1⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-126898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4-{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t+2,-2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $⟨0,-1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $⟨0,-1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨0,-1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨0,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨2t+2,-2t⟩ & =0 \\\\ 0(2t+2)+(-1)(-2t) & =0 \\\\ 2t & =0 \\\\ t & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=0.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨{0}^{2}+2(0),4-{0}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,4⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨0,-1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(0,4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-126898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "943d99b88dd5ef61db0015339755f4f0ea9e8b2c195300e13bfb7ee419247aea"} {"question" "q-126898" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-126898" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-1,3)"] ["math" "(0,4)"] ["math" "(3,3)"] ["text" "$(1,-4)$ and $(-1,3)$"] ["text" "$(3,3)$ and $(0,4)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-126898" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(0,4)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-75205" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}-1\\,,\\,{t}^{3}+1⟩,t\\in (-\\infty ,0)$ find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $⟨2\\,,\\,1⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-75205" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-1)\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t\\,,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors  $⟨2\\,,\\,1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $⟨2\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨2\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨2\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨2t\\,,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩ & =0 \\\\ 2\\cdot 2t+1\\cdot 3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 4t+3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ t(4+3t) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\in (-\\infty ,0)$, the only solution is $t=-\\frac{4}{3}$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-\\frac{4}{3}) & =⟨{(-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}-1\\,,\\,{(-\\frac{4}{3})}^{3}+1⟩=⟨\\frac{7}{9}\\,,\\,-\\frac{37}{27}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $⟨2\\,,\\,-1⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(\\frac{7}{9}\\,,\\,-\\frac{37}{27}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-75205" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "943d99b88dd5ef61db0015339755f4f0ea9e8b2c195300e13bfb7ee419247aea"} {"question" "q-75205" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-75205" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(0,0)"] ["math" "(\\frac{4}{3}\\,,\\,\\frac{16}{9})"] ["math" "(\\frac{7}{9}\\,,\\,-\\frac{37}{27})"] ["text" "$(0,0)$ and $(\\frac{4}{3}\\,,\\,\\frac{16}{9})$"] ["text" "$(3,-7)$ and $(\\frac{7}{9}\\,,\\,-\\frac{37}{27})$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-75205" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(\\frac{7}{9}\\,,\\,-\\frac{37}{27})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-164838" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $r(t)=(t+2)\\,i+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,j$, find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are perpendicular." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-164838" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+2)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1})\\,j \\\\  & =i+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ [(t+2)\\,i+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,j]\\cdot (i+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}\\,j) & =0 \\\\ (t+2)\\cdot 1+\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\cdot \\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}} & =0 \\\\ t+2+t & =0 \\\\ 2t+2 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=-1.$ \n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1)=(-1+2)\\,i+\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+1}\\,j=i+\\sqrt{2}\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(1,\\sqrt{2}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-164838" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "943d99b88dd5ef61db0015339755f4f0ea9e8b2c195300e13bfb7ee419247aea"} {"question" "q-164838" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-164838" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(0,\\sqrt{5})"] ["math" "(1,\\sqrt{2})"] ["math" "(2,1)"] ["text" "$(0,\\sqrt{5})$ and $(1,\\sqrt{2})$"] ["text" "$(1,\\sqrt{2})$ and $(2,1)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-164838" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(1,\\sqrt{2})"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-33498" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $r(t)=t\\,i+(1+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})\\,j$, find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are perpendicular." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-33498" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =i+t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ [t\\,i+(1+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})\\,j]\\cdot (i+t\\,j) & =0 \\\\ t\\cdot 1+(1+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2})\\cdot t & =0 \\\\ 2t+\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{3} & =0 \\\\ t(4+{t}^{2}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=0$ since $4+{t}^{2}>0$ for any $t.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0)=0\\,i+(1+\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2})\\,j=j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(0,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-33498" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "943d99b88dd5ef61db0015339755f4f0ea9e8b2c195300e13bfb7ee419247aea"} {"question" "q-33498" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-33498" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(0,1)"] ["math" "(2,3)"] ["text" "$(0,1)$ and $(2,3)$"] ["text" "$(0,1)$, $(2,2)$, and $(-2,2)$"] ["text" "$(2,3)$ and $(-2,3)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-33498" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(0,1)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-165041" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{4}+4t\\,,\\,{t}^{3}-2t\\,,\\,2{t}^{3}-3t⟩,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-165041" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}+4t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-3t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4{t}^{3}+4,\\,3{t}^{2}-2,\\,6{t}^{2}-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c\\,⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c\\,⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩ \\\\ ⟨4{t}^{3}+4,\\,3{t}^{2}-2,\\,6{t}^{2}-3⟩ & =c\\,⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩ \\\\ ⟨4{t}^{3}+4,\\,3{t}^{2}-2,\\,6{t}^{2}-3⟩ & =⟨0\\,,\\,c\\,,\\,3c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4{t}^{3}+4=0 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-2=c \\\\ 6{t}^{2}-3=3c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $t=-1.$ Substituting to the second equation, we get $c=1.$\nThese values also satisfy the third equation. \n\nTherefore, $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=-1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨(-1)^{4}+4(-1)\\,,\\,(-1)^{3}-2(-1)\\,,\\,2(-1)^{3}-3(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,\\,1,\\,1⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(-3,1,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-165041" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "943d99b88dd5ef61db0015339755f4f0ea9e8b2c195300e13bfb7ee419247aea"} {"question" "q-165041" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-165041" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-3,1,1)"] ["math" "(24,4,10)"] ["math" "(5,-1,-1)"] ["text" "$(0,0,0)$ and $(-3,1,1)$"] ["text" "$(5,-1,-1)$ and $(-3,1,1)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-165041" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(-3,1,1)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-165040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2}-2t\\,,\\,{t}^{2}\\,,\\,8t-2{t}^{2}⟩,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-165040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8t-2{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t-2,\\,2t,\\,8-4t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨0\\,,\\,1\\,,\\,3⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c\\,⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c\\,⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t-2,\\,2t,\\,8-4t⟩ & =c\\,⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t-2,\\,2t,\\,8-4t⟩ & =⟨c\\,,\\,2c\\,,\\,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2t-2=c \\\\ 2t=2c \\\\ 8-4t=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equation, we obtain that $t=2.$ Substituting to the second equation, we get $c=2.$\nThese values also satisfy the first equation. \n\nTherefore, $⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=2.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(2) & =⟨{2}^{2}-2(2)\\,,\\,{2}^{2}\\,,\\,8(2)-2(2)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,4,\\,8⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨1\\,,\\,2\\,,\\,0⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(0,4,8).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-165040" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "943d99b88dd5ef61db0015339755f4f0ea9e8b2c195300e13bfb7ee419247aea"} {"question" "q-165040" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-165040" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-1,1,6)"] ["math" "(0,4,8)"] ["math" "(3,1,-10)"] ["text" "$(0,0,0)$ and $(0,4,8)$"] ["text" "$(3,1,-10)$ and $(-1,1,6)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-165040" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(0,4,8)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-33517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{3}\\,,\\,{e}^{t}\\,,\\,{e}^{t-1}⟩$, find all points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-33517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t-1})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{t-1}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ ⟨{t}^{3}\\,,\\,{e}^{t}\\,,\\,{e}^{t-1}⟩ & =c\\,⟨3{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{t-1}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{t}^{3}=c(3{t}^{2}) \\\\ {e}^{t}=c{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t-1}=c{e}^{t-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we obtain that $c=1.$ Substituting $c=1$ into the first equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{3} & =3{t}^{2} \\\\ {t}^{3}-3{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}(t-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the points corresponding to $t=0$, and $t=3.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨{0}^{3},\\,{e}^{0},\\,{e}^{0-1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,{e}^{-1}⟩ \\\\ r(3) & =⟨{3}^{3},\\,{e}^{3},\\,{e}^{3-1}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨27,\\,{e}^{3},\\,{e}^{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the points $(0,1,{e}^{-1})$ and $(27,{e}^{3},{e}^{2}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-33517" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "943d99b88dd5ef61db0015339755f4f0ea9e8b2c195300e13bfb7ee419247aea"} {"question" "q-33517" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-33517" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(0,1,{e}^{-1})"] ["math" "(27,{e}^{3},{e}^{2})"] ["text" "$(0,1,0)$ and $(27,{e}^{3},{e}^{2})$"] ["text" "$(0,1,{e}^{-1})$ and $(27,{e}^{3},{e}^{2})$"] ["text" "$(1,0,{e}^{-1})$ and $(27,{e}^{3},{e}^{2})$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-33517" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(0,1,{e}^{-1})$ and $(27,{e}^{3},{e}^{2})$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-165042" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $r(t)={e}^{2t}\\,i+({t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j$, find all points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-165042" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\,i+({t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j & =c(2{e}^{2t}\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j) \\\\ {e}^{2t}\\,i+({t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j & =2c{e}^{2t}\\,i+c(2t+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $i$ and $j$ gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t}=2c{e}^{2t} \\\\ {t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2}=c(2t+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain $c=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting $c=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the second equality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}+t+\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(2t+1) \\\\ {t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ t & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel at the point corresponding to $t=0.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & ={e}^{0}\\,i+({0}^{2}+0+\\frac{1}{2})\\,j \\\\  & =i+\\frac{1}{2}\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(1,\\frac{1}{2}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-165042" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "943d99b88dd5ef61db0015339755f4f0ea9e8b2c195300e13bfb7ee419247aea"} {"question" "q-165042" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-165042" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(1,\\frac{1}{2})"] ["math" "(e,\\frac{5}{4})"] ["math" "({e}^{2},\\frac{5}{2})"] ["text" "$(1,\\frac{1}{2})$ and $({e}^{2},\\frac{5}{2})$"] ["text" "$(e,\\frac{5}{4})$ and $({e}^{-2},\\frac{1}{2})$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-165042" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(1,\\frac{1}{2})"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-33509" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $r(t)=t\\,i+(2+{t}^{3})\\,j+(1+{t}^{2})\\,k$, find all points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-33509" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2+{t}^{3})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+{t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =i+\\,3{t}^{2}\\,j+\\,2t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ t\\,i+(2+{t}^{3})\\,j+(1+{t}^{2})\\,k & =c(i+\\,3{t}^{2}\\,j+\\,2t\\,k).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $i,j,$ and $k$ gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}t=c \\\\ 2+{t}^{3}=c(3{t}^{2}) \\\\ 1+{t}^{2}=c(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation we obtain $t=c.$ Substituting $c=t$ into the second equality gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+{t}^{3} & =3{t}^{3} \\\\ 2 & =2{t}^{3} \\\\ {t}^{3} & =1 \\\\ t & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis satisfies also the equation $1+{t}^{2}=c(2t).$\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =i+(2+{1}^{3})\\,j+(1+{1}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =i+3\\,j+2\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $P(1,3,2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-33509" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "943d99b88dd5ef61db0015339755f4f0ea9e8b2c195300e13bfb7ee419247aea"} {"question" "q-33509" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-33509" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(0,2,1)"] ["math" "(1,3,2)"] ["math" "(2,10,5)"] ["text" "$(0,2,1)$ and $(-1,1,2)$"] ["text" "$(1,3,2)$ and $(-1,3,2)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "q-33509" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(1,3,2)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/state.json" "file_sha256" "1d42dd5b0cbb03f6ec2e7217f988790a10c07d7788945c5b7f2ec7f1c19302fe"} {"question" "e-12567" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a tangent vector to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=t\\,\\mathbf i+t^2\\,\\mathbf j+t^3\\,\\mathbf k$ at the point $P(1,1,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/example-12567.json" "file_sha256" "acbb4983ea29d3647cd46c3a42c28529a92bb49d1ea461facd172002181300e2"} {"question" "e-12567" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The point $P(1,1,1)$ is at the tip of $\\mathbf r(t)$ for some specific value $t_0$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point,  the vector $\\mathbf r'(t_0)$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\,i+\\,j+\\,k \\\\ t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+{t}^{3}\\,k & =i+\\,j+\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf i, \\mathbf j,$ and $\\mathbf k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}t=1 \\\\ {t}^{2}=1 \\\\ {t}^{3}=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=1.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,1,1)$ is at the tip of the vector $\\mathbf r(1).$  \n\nNow, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,k \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =i+2(1)\\,j+3(1)^{2}\\,k \\\\  & =i+2\\,j+3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at at the point $P(1,1,1)$ is $\\,\\mathbf i+2\\,\\mathbf j+3\\,\\mathbf k.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/assets/bae639af7c4267014af4e558f9a21b042470a3f7d46fa7a31ac0162c7b08ef08.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/example-12567.json" "file_sha256" "acbb4983ea29d3647cd46c3a42c28529a92bb49d1ea461facd172002181300e2"} {"question" "e-12568" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find a parametrization of the tangent line to the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle \\cos{t}, \\: \\sin{t},\\: 4t^2\\right\\rangle$ at the point where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/example-12568.json" "file_sha256" "fe5d67e2503a9658796aa1d95b2557c7ef14de22b544ffd15f15be6c6babe010"} {"question" "e-12568" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let  $P$ be the point at the tip of the vector $\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right ).$  At this point,  the vector $\\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right )$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(u)=\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right )+u \\, \\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right ), \\qquad u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,4{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}⟩=⟨0,1,{\\pi }^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,8t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,8(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,4\\pi ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the given curve at the point $P$ where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(u)=⟨0,1,{\\pi }^{2}⟩+u⟨-1,0,4\\pi ⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/assets/e24b811fb8d9e43bd0682e3686f8b2ef1198e36016978ca305a7df4dffe21889.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/example-12568.json" "file_sha256" "fe5d67e2503a9658796aa1d95b2557c7ef14de22b544ffd15f15be6c6babe010"} {"question" "e-12580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t^2+1\\,,\\, 6t+3\\right\\rangle,$ find all of the points at which the tangent to the curve is perpendicular to the vector $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1\\right\\rangle.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/example-12580.json" "file_sha256" "4eef03666effb06cc7ab81ab916550691d098899a1682df4b04f56d030b7dd88"} {"question" "e-12580" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1)\\,,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t+3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t\\,,\\,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors  $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1 \\right\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf r'(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1 \\right\\rangle \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t) = 0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨3\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ⟨3\\,,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨2t\\,,\\,6⟩ & =0 \\\\ 3\\cdot 2t+1\\cdot 6 & =0 \\\\ 6t+6 & =0 \\\\ t & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the only solution is $t=-1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(-1) & =⟨(-1)^{2}+1\\,,\\,6(-1)+3⟩=⟨2\\,,\\,-3⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\left\\langle 3\\,,\\, 1\\right\\rangle$ and $\\mathbf r'(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(2,-3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/example-12580.json" "file_sha256" "4eef03666effb06cc7ab81ab916550691d098899a1682df4b04f56d030b7dd88"} {"question" "e-12569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=(t^2+t)\\,\\mathbf i+(t^2-t)\\,\\mathbf j,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/example-12569.json" "file_sha256" "106c1f452a9b16f4bc400a26755bfe779bdd19a193ba87eef63fba83333c28cb"} {"question" "e-12569" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,j \\\\  & =(2t+1)\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $\\mathbf r'(t)=c(3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2t+1)\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j=c(3\\,i+\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ gives the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2t+1=3c \\\\ 2t-1=c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $c=2t-1.$ Substituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t+1 & =3c \\\\ 2t+1 & =3(2t-1) \\\\ 2t+1 & =6t-3 \\\\ 6t-2t & =1+3 \\\\ 4t & =4 \\\\ t & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf r'$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =({1}^{2}+1)\\,i+({1}^{2}-1)\\,j=2\\,i,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel at the point $(2,0).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/assets/0f723e89b1c91367d6488e034f8b627b3c406aed6d8d1ffc244d6f014021e04e.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14078039/example-12569.json" "file_sha256" "106c1f452a9b16f4bc400a26755bfe779bdd19a193ba87eef63fba83333c28cb"}] "usage" {"q-32916" [] "q-33461" [] "e-12580" [] "q-165042" [] "q-33498" [] "q-33509" [] "e-12567" [] "e-12568" [] "q-126835" [] "q-126739" [] "q-126838" [] "e-12569" []} "basis" 2813 "retractions" [[17592186119436 :question/worked-solution "The point $P(1,1,1)$ is at the tip of $\\mathbf r(t)$ for some specific value $t_0$ of the parameter $t.$ At this point,  the vector $\\mathbf r'(t_0)$ is tangent to the curve.\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to $P.$ We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\,i+\\,j+\\,k \\\\ t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+{t}^{3}\\,k & =i+\\,j+\\,k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand equating the coefficients of $\\mathbf i, \\mathbf j,$ and $\\mathbf k$ yields the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}t=1 \\\\ {t}^{2}=1 \\\\ {t}^{3}=1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get $t=1.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,1,1)$ is at the tip of the vector $\\mathbf r(1).$  \n\nNow, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=1\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,k \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =i+2(1)\\,j+3(1)^{2}\\,k \\\\  & =i+2\\,j+3\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a tangent vector to the given curve at at the point $P(1,1,1)$ is $\\,\\mathbf i+2\\,\\mathbf j+3\\,\\mathbf k.$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/assets/bae639af7c4267014af4e558f9a21b042470a3f7d46fa7a31ac0162c7b08ef08.png)"] [17592186119437 :question/worked-solution "Let  $P$ be the point at the tip of the vector $\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right ).$  At this point,  the vector $\\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right )$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf R(u)=\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right )+u \\, \\mathbf r'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right ), \\qquad u \\in (-\\infty, \\infty).\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $\\mathbf r\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) \\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,4{(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{2}⟩=⟨0,1,{\\pi }^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow,  we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and evaluate it at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,8t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}),\\,8(\\frac{\\pi }{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,0,4\\pi ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the tangent line to the given curve at the point $P$ where $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(u)=⟨0,1,{\\pi }^{2}⟩+u⟨-1,0,4\\pi ⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/assets/e24b811fb8d9e43bd0682e3686f8b2ef1198e36016978ca305a7df4dffe21889.png)"] [17592186119438 :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-t)\\,j \\\\  & =(2t+1)\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $\\mathbf r'(t)=c(3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2t+1)\\,i+(2t-1)\\,j=c(3\\,i+\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\mathbf i$ and $\\mathbf j$ gives the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2t+1=3c \\\\ 2t-1=c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $c=2t-1.$ Substituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2t+1 & =3c \\\\ 2t+1 & =3(2t-1) \\\\ 2t+1 & =6t-3 \\\\ 6t-2t & =1+3 \\\\ 4t & =4 \\\\ t & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\mathbf r'$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =({1}^{2}+1)\\,i+({1}^{2}-1)\\,j=2\\,i,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $3\\, \\mathbf i+\\,\\mathbf j$ are parallel at the point $(2,0).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14049728/assets/0f723e89b1c91367d6488e034f8b627b3c406aed6d8d1ffc244d6f014021e04e.png)"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186117391 17592186117392 17592186117393 17592186117394 17592186117395 17592186117637 17592186117638 17592186117639 17592186117640 17592186117641 17592186327997 17592186327998 17592186327999 17592186328000 17592186328001 17592186328004 17592186328005 17592186328006 17592186328007 17592186328008 17592186328046 17592186328047 17592186328048 17592186328049 17592186328050 17592186328074 17592186328075 17592186328076 17592186328077 17592186328078 17592186328088 17592186328089 17592186328090 17592186328091 17592186328092 17592186347755 17592186347756 17592186347757 17592186347758 17592186347759] "immutable_fields" [[17592186117390 :answer-field/correct] [17592186117390 :answer-field/id] [17592186117390 :answer-field/key] [17592186117390 :answer-field/type] [17592186117636 :answer-